7.9.4 Deimos, August 30, 2026

- Simple `numarray` read access with standard indexing and `numarray.subarray` has become at least 5 % faster.

- `fractals.draw` when given no colouring information now uses the default Fractint colour map instead of blue hues.

- `fractals.draw` with the 'map' option now accepts FRACTINT colour map files that include comments introduced with a hash
  or a semicolon. Blank lines will now be simply ignored, too.

- `fractals.draw` also accepts a colour map table of 256 RGB values instead of the name of a FRACTINT *.map file. You can
  use the new `fractals.genmap` function to create such a colour map table. Define the colour stops:

  > anchors := [
  >    [0, 0, 255],   # Blue
  >    [0, 255, 0],   # Green
  >    [255, 0, 0]    # Red
  > ]

  and pass them to `fractals.genmap`:

  > import fractals;

  > fractals.draw(fractals.markmandel, -0.5, 0, 1.5, res = 1280:768, map = fractals.genmap(anchors));

  If you want to use colours defined in the gdi.COLOURS table, then multiply each component by 255 to convert it from the
  0 .. 1 to the 0 .. 255 range.

  > anchors := [
  >    gdi.COLOURS.bordeaux, gdi.COLOURS.spring, gdi.COLOURS.spring,
  >    gdi.COLOURS.yellow, gdi.COLOURS.yellow,
  >    gdi.COLOURS.white, gdi.COLOURS.white, gdi.COLOURS.white,
  > ]

  > for i, j in anchors do
  >    anchors[i] := map(<: x -> x*255 :>, j);
  > od;

  > fractals.genmap(anchors, 'bordeaux_spring.map');
  Successfully generated file `bordeaux_spring.map`.

- New `fractals.sortmap` sorts a colour map non-destructively and either returns the new sorted table or writes it to a file.
  You can sort by hue, saturation or step. Example:

  > fractals.sortmap(
  >   fractals.genmap(anchors),
  >   mode   = 'saturation',
  >   output = 'mymap.map',
  >   header = '# my first saturation-sorted colour map');

- New `fractals.readmap` reads in a FRACTINT colour map file, and new `fractals.writemap` writes one to disk.


7.9.3 Deimos, August 28, 2026

- `pushd` can now push booleans into a cache stack (stack #7).

- `stack.insertd`, `stack.replaced` and `stack.enqueued` could push functions and unsafe strings into the cache stack (stack #7),
  causing invalid reads. This has been fixed. The functions can now only insert numbers, complex numbers, booleans and strings into stack #7.


7.9.2 Deimos, August 25, 2026

- `strings.instr`: If the plain option has been set to `false`, then the function nevertheless conducted a plain search. This
  has been fixed. The function, in default pattern matching mode, has also been sped up by a factor of 7.

- The `atendof` operator has become around 10 % faster.

- In some locales, string comparison with the relational `<`, `<=`, `>`, and `>=` operators could fail or even crash Agena with
  strings containing embedded zeros. This has been fixed.

- An abnormally massive number of function arguments will no longer cause segmentation faults.

- This release has been successfully Valgrind-tested on x86 Linux.


7.9.1 Deimos, August 24, 2026

- New `suffix.gfind` returns an iterator function that, each time it is called, returns the position of the next
  match of a pattern in a string.

  > corpus := 'banana split, banana bread, banana shake';

  > sabin := suffix.sa(corpus);

  > f := suffix.gfind(corpus, sabin, 'Banana', 1, true)

  > f():
  1

  > f():
  15

  > f():
  29

  > f():
  null

- New `suffix.save` saves an input string, Suffix Array and optionally a Longest Common Prefix array to disk, into
  just one file. You can load them back again into a session with `suffix.read`.

- `suffix.strstr` has been renamed to `suffix.find`. An alias has been provided to ensure backward compatibility.

- You can now pass a fourth optional argument to `suffix.find` which specifies where to start the search for a
  substring. The default still is the start of a string.

  If you pass `true` as a fifth argument to the function, the search will be case-insensitive.

- `suffix.count` now allows for the position in the input string where to search the count.

- `str.lcs` has been protected against strings that are too large.

- This release has been successfully Dr. Memory-tested on Windows and Valgrind-tested on x64 Linux.


7.9.0 Deimos, August 23, 2026

- The new `suffix` library provides a high-performance interface to libdivsufsort, enabling fast suffix array and
  Longest Common Prefix (LCP) array generation for very large strings. Aside from two foundational primitives of the
  suffix engine, the package has functions to count substrings, search for substrings and their positions and find
  the shortest unique substring. It even provides basic autocomplete functionality:

  > corpus := 'banana split,banana bread,banana shake,apple pie,apricot tart,bandanna';

  > sabin  := suffix.sa(corpus);

  > suffix.autocomplete(corpus, sabin, 'ba', ','):
  [banana bread, banana shake, banana split, bandanna]

  For the increase in speed just one example: the search for three DNA subsequences inside a 1-megabyte DNA sequence
  string, repeated 100 times, is taking 2.47 seconds with `strings.find` and 1.43 seconds with equivalent `suffix.find`,
  a 40 % increase on an i7-9700K CPU running at 3.60GHz.

- New `strings.lrs` finds the largest repeating substring. Example:

  > strings.lrs('banana'):
  ana

- New `io.readto` reads a file until a specific marker character or until a validator function returns `true`.

- `strings.readfrom` by default no longer includes the marker in the result. You can pass `true` as a third argument if you
  want it included.

- `strings.jaro` has been boosted from 50 % (dissimilar strings) to 1000 % (equal strings).

- Fixed the underlying logic that computes the binomial coefficient n over k for negative integral n:

  - A bug where negative numbers were blocked by standard safety checks has been removed.
  - An overflow false alarm has been removed.

  This benefits `binomial`, `calc.smoothstep`, `combinat.catalan`, `combinat.numbcomb`, `stats.binompdf` and `stats.negbinompdf`.

- Added self-made FRACTINT colour maps to the binary installers that are not subject to the Stone Soup Group's strict copyright
  regulation. See the `share/fractint` folder of your local Agena installation. You can use them to colourise fractals created
  with the `fractals` package. For additional maps, check the SourceForge `Packages` download folder, file fractint.zip.


7.8.11 Phobos, August 22, 2026

- New `strings.readfrom` reads the remainder of a file starting from a specific marker character. Alternatively it reads all the
  characters once a validator function returns `true`.

- `strings.hirschberg` now also returns the total length of the edit sequence and the Damerau-Levenshtein distance between the
  two strings compared.

  Before, the function crashed Agena when given too large strings. This has been fixed.

- `strings.lcs` which searches for the largest common substring sometimes returned erroneous results and even crashed with
  larger strings. These issues have been resolved, too, and the function now fully observes the order of the characters in the
  strings.

- `strings.lcs`, `strings.dleven` and `strings.leven` have been tuned by a factor of at least 37 [sic !], making them suitable
  to process very large strings.

- This release has been successfully Valgrind-tested on x86 and x64 Linux.


7.8.10 Phobos, August 21, 2026

- The new function `strings.hirschberg` takes two strings s and t and returns their global alignment in three results:
  the edit sequence, the alignment of s and the alignment of t. The function uses the memory-friendly Hirschberg algorithm
  which yields a total structural space complexity of strictly O(N + M).

  > strings.hirschberg('Alex', 'Axelander'):
  =++=+++=!       A  l   ex       Axelander

  > strings.hirschberg('Axelander', 'Alex'):
  =--=---=!       Axelander       A  l   ex

- When given the -b, -q, -Q command-line options and with multiple returns, the pretty-printer wrote the last return only, and
  this multiple times. The same happened when setting the 'enclose', 'encloseback' or 'enclosedouble' switches to true via a
  call to `environ.kernel`. This has all been fixed.

- This release has been successfully Valgrind-tested on x86 Linux.


7.8.9 Phobos, August 19, 2026

- `astro.cdate` can now be called with no arguments and returns the current system date and time in UTC.

   > import astro;

   > astro.cdate():
   2026   8   19   0.77648148148148   18   38   8

- `astro.togast` has been renamed to `astro.siderial` and can now compute either Greenwich Apparent Sidereal Time (GAST) or
  Local Siderial Time if the first argument is the longitude in decimal degrees of the location of interest. An alias has been
  provided to ensure backward compatibility.

  > import astro;

  GAST:

  >  y, m, d, fd, hh, mm, ss := 2026, 8, 18, 9, 36, 20.000004172325

  > astro.siderial(y, m, d, hh, mm, ss):
  7.3902858764316

  Local Siderial Time for Bonn, Germany:

  > astro.siderial(7.0955, y, m, d, hh, mm, ss):
  7.8633192097649


7.8.8 Phobos, August 15, 2026

- New `astro.coords` converts between horizontal and equatorial coordinates.

- New `astro.togast` calculates Greenwich Apparent Sidereal Time (GAST).

- `astro.getposition` no longer returns the Julian Date. It still returns a table with azimuth, altitude, right ascension
  and declination data.

- `astro.cdate`, `astro.gethourangle`, `astro.getposition`, `astro.hdate`, `astro.pdate`, `astro.searchaltitude`,
  `astro.searchhourangle`, `astro.searchriseset` now preserve the fractional part of a second if given by the user.

- `astro.hdate` returned the wrong month with dates at the end of a Hebrew year that is not a leap year. Also, edge case
  dates have been fixed. The Hebrew month enumeration has been changed, too, see the Agena Reference.

- Roundoff errors in `astro.pdate` have been reduced.

- Many `astro` functions issue an error if the input data is out-of-range, for example `February 30` or `latitude = 181`.


7.8.7 Phobos, August 13, 2026

- New `astro.getposition` calculates the exact position of a celestial body for a specific location and time.

  Example for the Sun over Chattanooga, TN, for August 13, 2026, noon local time:

  > import astro;

  > lat, lon, elev := 35.0456, -85.3097, 206;

  > astro.getposition('Sun', lat, lon, elev, 2026, 8, 13, 17, 46, 6):
  2461266.2403472 [altitude ~ 69.454648517606, azimuth ~ 179.99942552291, declination ~ 14.493921704599, ra ~ 9.559571062088]

  The function also supports the Moon and the nine planets of the Solar System.

- New `astro.searchriseset` searches for the next time a celestial body rises or sets as seen by an observer on the Earth.
  Examples for sunrise (last argument is +1) and sunset (last argument is -1) at Chattanooga on August 13, 2026, enter:

  > import astro;

  > lat, lon, elev := 35.0456, -85.3097, 206;

  > astro.searchriseset('Sun', lat, lon, elev, 2026, 8, 13, 0, 0, 0, +1):
  2461265.9582471 [2026, 8, 13, 10, 59, 52.548474669456]

  > astro.searchriseset('Sun', lat, lon, elev, 2461266, -1):
  2461266.5220485 [2026, 8, 14, 0, 31, 44.994656145573]

  The function supports the Moon and the nine planets of the Solar System, too.

- New `astro.searchhourangle` searches for the time when the center of a body reaches a specified hour angle.

  Example: For the solar noon, with the starting date August 13, 2026 00:00:00 UTC, at location Chattanooga, TN, we have:

  > import astro;

  > lat, lon, angle, dir := 35.0456, -85.3097, 0.0, 1.0;

  > astro.searchhourangle('Sun', lat, lon, 0, 2026, 8, 13, 0, 0, 0, angle, dir):
  2461266.2403325 [altitude ~ 69.456447100381, azimuth ~ 179.99998731165, declination ~ 14.502047100381, ra ~ 9.5592033742828]

  The function supports the Moon and the nine planets of the Solar System, as well.

- New `astro.gethourangle` finds the hour angle of a body for a given observer and time. For Chattanooga:

  > import astro;

  > lat, lon, angle, dir := 35.0456, -85.3097, 0.0, 1.0;

  > astro.gethourangle('Sun', lat, lon, 0, 2026, 8, 13, 12, 0, 0):
  18.231160842525

  The function also supports the Moon and the nine planets of the Solar System.

- `astro.winter` did not return a correct date and the code to compute the seasons in general was way too complicated. So all
  seasonal finding functions have been replaced by one new unified function `astro.season` which returns the start of spring,
  summer, autumn and winter as a Julian Date:

  > import astro;

  > astro.seasons(2026):
  [autumn ~ 2461306.5039192, spring ~ 2461120.1150005, summer ~ 2461212.8506981, winter ~ 2461396.3683124]

  Aliases to `astro.spring`, `astro.summer`, `astro.autumn`, `astro.winter` have been provided for backward-compatibility.

- The Julian Date and the calendar date and time returned by `astro.searchriseset` differed slightly. This has been fixed.


7.8.6 Phobos, August 12, 2026

- New `astro.searchmoonphase` calculates the exact time a specific lunar phase occurs, from a given starting point.
  Examples for two succeeeding Full Moons:

  > import astro;

  > astro.searchmoonphase(limit = -40, longitude = 180):  # search backward
  2461251.1094261 [2026, 7, 29, 14, 36, 19.011207818985]

  > astro.searchmoonphase(2461252, limit = 40, longitude = 180):  # search forward
  2461280.6808049 [2026, 8, 28, 4, 19, 6.085461974144]

  > astro.searchmoonphase([2026, 7, 30, 12, 0, 0], limit = 40, longitude = 180):  # dito
  2461280.6808049, [2026, 8, 28, 4, 19, 6.085461974144]

- New `astro.searchplanetapsis` finds the very next occurring Perihelion (the point in a planet's orbit closest to the Sun)
  or Aphelion (the point furthest from the Sun) relative to a given starting date and time. Examples:

  > import astro;

  Search for the next event for the ninth planet:

  > astro.searchplanetapsis('Pluto', [2026, 8, 11, 12, 0, 0]):
  2114  2  20  10  45  39.279797673225  aphelion  49.32025774638  2493232.9483713

  And the event after that:

  > astro.searchplanetapsis('Pluto', 2493233):
  2237  9  16  17  1  52.103931605816  perihelion  29.645264444715  2538366.2096308

- New `astro.searchlunarapsis` finds the very next occurring Lunar Apogee (the point in the Moon's orbit furthest from Earth)
  or Lunar Perigee (the point closest to Earth) relative to a given starting date and time.

  > import astro;

  Search for the next event from the current date:

  > L := [astro.searchlunarapsis()]:
  [2026, 8, 22, 8, 20, 46.747706830502, apogee, 404632.01214081, 2461274.8477633]

  Search for the event after that:

  > astro.searchlunarapsis(L[9]):
  2026  9  6  20  44  8.9950999617577  perigee  368248.59424929  2461290.363993

- New `astro.lunarlibration` calculates the lunar libration (apparent physical `wobble`) and sub-Earth tracking coordinates
  of the Moon for a specific moment.

- New `astro.illumination` calculates the apparent brightness, illuminated surface area percentage, and phase geometry
  of a celestial body as seen from Earth at a specific moment. For Saturn, it also returns the tilt angle of its ring system.

  > astro.illumination('Moon', [2026, 8, 28, 4, 19, 6.085461974144]):
  [fraction ~ 0.99998342840944, magnitude ~ -12.647142713872, phaseangle ~ 0.46648311109478]

- You can now pass year, month, day and time (UTC) as individual arguments to `astro.peak` which searches for the date and
  time Venus will next appear brightest.

- If no argument is given to `astro.moonphase` then the current date and hour is taken to compute the result. The function
  alternatively accepts the Julian Date instead of a Gregorian date.

- `astro.searchalt` has been renamed to `astro.searchaltitude` and the function now returns the Julian Date of the event
  as a first result and the corresponding Gregorian date in a table as the second result. You may have to change your code.

  > import astro;

  > astro.searchaltitude('Sun', 31.7683, 35.2137, 754.0, 2026, 8, 15, 12, 0, 0, -8.5, -1):
  2461268.2091674 [2026, 8, 15, 17, 1, 12.062306106091]

  The function also alternatively takes a Julian Date instead of a Gregorian date, so the following two statements are
  equivalent:

  > astro.searchaltitude('Sun', 31.7683, 35.2137, 754.0, 2461268, -8.5, -1):
  2461268.2091674 [2026, 8, 15, 17, 1, 12.062306106091]

  > astro.searchaltitude('Sun', 31.7683, 35.2137, 754.0, 2026, 8, 15, 12, 0, 0, -8.5, -1):
  2461268.2091674 [2026, 8, 15, 17, 1, 12.062306106091]

- `astro.jdate` has been patched to properly process fractional seconds and to properly acknowledge milliseconds.


7.8.5 Phobos, August 10, 2026

- New `astro.searchalt` calculates the exact timestamp when a celestial body reaches a specific altitude angle relative to an
  observer's horizon. Sun, Moon and the planets of the Solar System are supported.

  Example: Find when the Sun drops to 8.5 degrees below the horizon in Jerusalem, marking nightfall on Saturday evening,
  August 15, 2026, searching from 12:00:00 UTC:

  > y, m, d, hh, mm, ss := astro.searchalt('Sun', 31.7683, 35.2137, 754.0,
  >    2026, 8, 15, 12, 0, 0, -8.5, -1)

  > printf('Shabbat ends at %04d-%02d-%02d %02d:%02d:%02d UTC.', y, m, d, hh, mm, ss)
  Shabbat ends at 2026-08-15 17:01:12 UTC.

- New `astro.shabbat` computes the start and end of Shabbat for a given year and calendar week.

- The functionality of `astro.jd` has been included in `astro.jdate` and the former function has been removed. Just add
  the new `full = true` option in the call to get the three results that `astro.jd` once returned.

  > astro.jdate(2026, 8, 9, 12, 30, 1, full = true):
  2461262.0208449 2461261 45001000

- You can now pass a Gregorian date and time to `astro.hdate`:

  > astro.hdate([2026, 8, 10, 12, 0, 0]):
  5786    12      27      0.5     12      0       0

  If you want the return in a table, now always pass `true` as the very last argument to the function.

- You can now pass the Hebrew year, month, day and time in a table to `astro.h2jd`.

- Likewise, `astro.p2jd` accepts the Persian year, month, day, etc. in a table, and `astro.pdate` accepts the Gregorian
  year, month, day, etc. in a table, as well.


7.8.4 Phobos, August 09, 2026

- New `astro.jd` returns the integral Julian Date, the milliseconds since midnight and the Julian Date incl.
  milliseconds-since-noon in its fractional part.

- `xbase.writetime` now correctly writes timestamps (dBASE 7 `@` data type) to a dBASE file, without the date always
  one day into the future.

- `os.date` and `strings.strptime` ignored the '%s' specifier to extract milliseconds. This has been fixed. The '%Q'
  specifier, which also extracts milliseconds, has been added to the documentation.

- `strings.strftime` has been hardened internally.

- The DOS edition has now been compiled with DJGPP/GCC 14.2.0 and latest GMP, MPFR libraries.


7.8.3 Phobos, August 05, 2026

- Implemented architectural changes to allow Windows and UNIX Agena dynamically linked libraries to be used in third-party
  projects again.

- The help windows in AgenaEdit are now properly spaced and no longer swallow text.


7.8.2 Phobos, August 04, 2026

- AgenaEdit for Windows, 32-bit x86 Debian and 64-bit x64 Debian has now been built with the latest FLTK 1.4.5 library.

- AgenaEdit for Solaris has been updated for FLTK 1.3.8 and AgenaEdit for macOS has been updated for FLTK 1.4.4.

- Fixed the no-argument feature of `os.drivestat` which failed to work in UNIX/macOS.


7.8.2 Phobos, August 02, 2026

- Updated `os.drivestat` across Windows, OS/2, and DOS, to streamline the return:

  - Added the new 'label' field in DOS to return the volume label.
  - Added the new 'driveletter' field (including a trailing colon) in DOS and Windows.
  - Unified the 'drivetype' field in OS/2 by merging 'filesystem' and 'iType' into a more readable description
    ('ResidentCharacter', 'PseudoCharacter', 'Fixed', 'Remote', 'CD-ROM', or 'RAMDISK'), aligning it with the
    DOS and Windows output.
  - Changed the behaviour in OS/2 to return `fail` instead of raising an error when an unassigned drive letter
    is passed, matching DOS and Windows.
  - Standardised error handling in Windows to raise an error when an invalid drive letter (e.g., '2' or '2:')
    is provided, matching the existing OS/2 and DOS behaviour.
  - Added the new 'isfat32' boolean field in OS/2 and Windows to indicate FAT32 file systems.
  - Fixed the no-argument feature which failed to work in OS/2 and DOS.

  And added special credits to eCSoft/2 Italy and to two Rosetta Code contributors to the Agena Primer.


7.8.1 Phobos, August 01, 2026

- New `os.getcommandline` returns the complete command-line string used to launch the current Agena process, including the
  executable name and all passed flags or arguments. Example in Windows:

  C:\agena\src>chcp 65001

  C:\agena\src>agena -p c:/agena/lib-éñ/

  > os.getcommandline():
  agena  -p c:/agena/lib-éñ/

  The function is available on all supported platforms.

- `os.getprocesses` has been improved and now correctly returns the number of open handles.

- `os.drivestat` now returns the maximum number of characters allowed for a single folder name or filename with the new
  field 'maxnamelength' and the total absolute path limit with the new field 'maxpathlength'.

  In Windows, the function returns various flags such as unicode support, disk quotas, sparse files, compressed
  volume, read-only volume, etc. in the new 'flags' field plus NTFS version information in the new 'ntfsver' field.

  The function is now also available on macOS.

  You no longer need to pass a drive letter (OS/2, DOS, Windows) or path (UNIX) to `os.drivestat`. If called
  without arguments or just with `null`, the function automatically determines the `current drive`.

  Example on Windows:

  > os.drivestat():
  [bytespersector ~ 512, dosdevice ~ \Device\HarddiskVolume11, drivetype ~ Fixed, filesystem ~ NTFS,
  flags ~ {ACL support, Encrypted File System (EFS) support, Update Sequence Number (USN) journals,
  case-sensitive file names, compression per file, disk quotas, extended attributes, hard links,
  named streams, object identifiers, opening of files per file identifier, preserved case of file names,
  reparse points, sparse files, transactions, unicode file names}, freeclusters ~ 12584670,
  freesize ~ 51546808320, label ~ drive_c, maxnamelength ~ 255, ntfsver ~ 3.1, sectorspercluster ~ 8,
  serialnumber ~ 845194224, totalclusters ~ 243926527, totalsize ~ 999123054592, trim ~ true,
  usedsize ~ 947576246272]

- On Windows only, new `os.bios` reads out BIOS and motherboard information.

  > os.bios():
  [BIOSReleaseDate ~ 09/07/2021, BIOSVendor ~ American Megatrends Inc., BIOSVersion ~ 3101,
   BaseBoardManufacturer ~ ASUSTeK COMPUTER INC., BaseBoardProduct ~ ROGSTRIX B360-F GAMING,
   BaseBoardVersion ~ Rev 1.xx, BiosMajorRelease ~ 5, BiosMinorRelease ~ 13, SystemManufacturer ~ ASUS,
   SystemProductName ~ System Product Name, SystemSKU ~ ASUS_MB_CNL, SystemSerialNumber ~ unknown]

- On macOS, Linux and Solaris, `os.memstate` now returns the fields `freevirtual`, `totalvirtual`,
  `freepagefile` and `totalpagefile`.

- On Windows, Linux, macOS and Solaris, `os.vga` returns the name of the primary GPU:

  > os.vga():
  [depth ~ 32, dimension ~ 43:80, monitors ~ 1, primarygpu ~ NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1650,
   resolution ~ 1920:1080, vrefresh ~ 60]

  The function is now fully supported on Linux and macOS. The Solaris version has also been extended except for the
  vertical refresh rate.

- In OS/2, `os.getmodulefilename` could theoretically crash Agena in case of an internal error. This has been fixed.

- There had been multiple issues with Agena in Windows 2000, 2000 Server, 2003 Server and XP: `Procedure entry point rand_s
  not found` errors and the `gdi` and `curses` packages not running due to missing or incompatible dependency files. This has
  all been fixed.

- Furthermore, the portable Windows installers included a launcher file `run.exe` that could not start Agena if the dependencies
  libmingwex-4.dll, libgcc_s_dw2-1.dll, libexpat-1.dll and libpcre2-8-0.dll were not already in the PATH. This issue has been
  resolved, too, as has the faulty `run.bat` script been fixed.

- Fixed some scripts and accompanying batch files in the share/scripting folder. Also removed various problems with the RASE
  derivative in Windows and OS/2 and with scripts accompanying the srglue/sragena tools that create executable files from
  Agena scripts.

- The Intel and ARM Debian installers now correctly set the permissions of the `astro` location database files.

- This release has been successfully Dr. Memory-tested on Windows and Valgrind-tested on x86 and x64 Linux.


7.8.0 Phobos, July 25, 2026

- The `in` and `notin` operators now support string hash sets provided by the `strhash` package.

- New `strhash.has` checks for the existence of a string.

- The default check in `dblhash.has` been changed from approximate equality to exact equality.

- The `intmap` package has been extended and now supports unsigned integer keys and unsigned integer values, too.
  The default is still the signed-integer-key-to-signed-integer-value mode, with the integers in the range -2,147,483,648 =
  -2^31 to 2,147,483,647 = 2^31-1, so you do not have to change your code.

  If you just define

  > h := intmap.new(signed = false)

  then you can use non-negative integers in the range 0 to 4,294,967,295 = 2^32-1.

  > intmap.include(h, 0, 2^32-1)
  > intmap.include(h, 2^32-1, 0)
  > intmap.totable(h):
  [0 ~ 4294967295, 4294967295 ~ 0]

  With this solution, you do not need to explicitly remap keys or values.

- New `intmap.remap` converts an integral Agena number in the range 0 to 4,294,967,295 (2^32-1) to -2,147,483,648 (-2^31)
  to 2,147,483,647 (2^31-1) and vice versa.

- The `intmap` package is now part of the Agena core and no longer needs to be initialised with the `import` statement.

- The new `dblmap` package implements number-to-number hash maps useful for storing very large time series and other
  floating-point-based data. Most package functions can be used in an OOP style. `dblmap` hash maps consume only 40% of the
  memory that native tables arrays do, with insertions running 50% faster. However, lookups and deletions are 33% slower
  than with native table arrays.

- In Windows, OS/2 and Linux, `os.getmodulefilename` worked but it was unsafe. This has been fixed.

- In Windows 10 and 11, the following functions could - theoretically - read invalid memory and thus fail determining
  the correct Windows version. Although this has never shown up with production binaries, the issue has been resolved for
  all the affected functions, which are: `os.battery`, `os.computername`, `os.cpuinfo`, `os.cpuload`, `os.fstat`, `os.ftok`,
  `os.getadapter`, `os.getip`, `os.getlocale`, `os.getmac`, `os.getprocesses`, `os.getusbdevices`, `os.inode`, `os.issysdir`,
  `os.memstate`, `os.monitor`, `os.mousestate`, `os.netdomain`, `os.netuse`, `os.searchpath`, `os.system`, `os.terminate`,
  `os.uptime`, `os.xgetbv` and `strings.format`. The `agena.exe` startup binary has also been fixed just in case.

- The Dr. Memory 2.6.0 cfg file has been updated to suppress non-Agena-related warnings on Windows 10 and 11. The Windows
  sections of the makefiles have been changed for Dr. Memory to potentially find more bugs.

- This release has been successfully Dr. Memory-tested on Windows and Valgrind-tested on x64 Linux.


7.7.10 Ariel, July 23, 2026

- In the `strmap`, `strhash` and `uintmap` packages, patched the underlying khash library to the use the CRC32 hash instead of
  the DJB2/BKDR polynomial hash variant, reducing collisions - if occurring at all - from 0.05599 % to 0.00637 %, or by 89 %,
  at a speed penalty of 10 %, with 517,996 surnames taken as a reference.

- `dblhash.has` in approximate mode now internally first tries to look up a number with exact equality before linearly searching
  the entire hash set. This dramatically reduces look-up times while guaranteeing an approximate scan if needed.

- Resolved a theoretically latent `use-after-free` vulnerability in the hash maps implemented in the `strmap`, `strhash`,
  `intmap` and `uintmap` packages, by fixing the respective garbage collecting '__gc' metamethods.

- This release has been successfully Dr. Memory-tested on Windows and Valgrind-tested on x64 Linux.


7.7.9 Ariel, July 22, 2026

- The `dblmap` package has been replaced by the new `uintmap` package which implements unsigned 4-byte integer-to-string
  hash maps and are two to three times faster than the previous double-to-string implementation. The new package is built-in
  so you do not have to initialise it with the `import` statement. Most `uintmap` functions can be called OOP-style. Example:

  > h := uintmap.new()
  > h@@include(10, 'ten')
  > h@@include(20, 'twenty')
  > h[10], h[20]:
  ten     twenty
  > h@@purge(20)
  > h[20]:
  null
  > f := h@@iterate()
  > f():
  10      ten
  > f():
  null

- Most `intmap` functions can now be called OOP-style, too.

- `intmap.include` and `strmap.include` didn't return a Boolean but just returned nothing. This has been fixed.

- This release has been successfully Dr. Memory-tested on Windows and Valgrind-tested on x64 Linux.


7.7.8 Ariel, July 20, 2026

- Added the new function `strings.lpad` which prepends characters to the beginning of a string (left-padding). The function
  is actually an alias to `strings.rjustify`.

- Likewise, new `strings.rpad` appends characters to the end of a string (right-padding). The function is an alias to
  `strings.ljustify`.

- `strings.ljustify` and `strings.rjustify` have been rewritten in C and have become 63 % faster.

- With bitfields created by the `memfile` package, the pretty-printer now prints the individual bytes from the left end
  of the field to the right end. This is to avoid confusion about the actual location of a bit. Also improved the description
  of `memfile.clearbit`, `memfile.getbit`, `memfile.getbyte`, `memfile.setbit` in the Reference.

- Tweaked `subs`, `subsop` a tiny bit.

- If a function has a metatable assigned, `environ.attrib` can now successfully extract the `__weak` field if present and
  no longer reports `fail` with the `weak` field.

- `environ.arity`, `environ.attrib` and `stack.mapd` could cause invalid memory reads if a function either has an empty
  parameter list or has been called with no arguments. The functions also corrupted the stack. This has all been fixed.

- In the Primer, redacted the explanation of weak tables in Chapter 6.20, now also featuring correct examples.

- This release has been successfully Dr. Memory-tested on Windows and Valgrind-tested on x64 Linux.


7.7.7 Ariel, July 17, 2026

- Fixes for out-of-memory situations to `dlong.new`, `io.infile`, `io.nlines`, `io.readfile`, `io.readlines`, `io.skiplines`,
  `mapm` package, `union`, `minus` and `intersect` metamethods of the `numarray` package, `numarray.unique`, `pushd` statement,
  `os.readlink`, `stack` package, `stats.mode`, `stats.sorted`, `strings.hextodec`, `strings.tolatin` and the `xbase` package.
  The patches prevent corruption of data in case of failed memory re-allocation.

- Also introduced out-of-memory management for Agena initialisation, `binio.readbytes`, `environ.kernel`, `io.lines`,
  `memfile.append`, `memfile.charbuf`, `memfile.prepend`, `mprint`, `net.accept`, `net.bind`, `net.connect`, `net.listen`,
  `net.open`, `os.getip`, `os.islocale`, `os.symlink`, `print`, `rsorted`, `stack.readbytes`, `stack.readbytes`, `str.fields`,
  `strhash.include`, `strings.iterate`, `strmap.include`, `utils.udata`.


7.7.6 Ariel, July 16, 2026

- If you commit on the prompt or in the code:

  > environ.kernel(nulliszero = true)

  then in basic arithmetic only (+, -, *, /, ^, ** operators) `null` will be interpreted as zero. Default is `false` to
  facilitate debugging of code as before.

- New `fractals.ifs` is a generalized Iterated Function System (IFS) engine designed to compute and plot self-similar
  fractals - such as the Barnsley Fern or Fractal Trees - using dynamic, randomized affine transformations.

- In the Reference, fully documented `fractals.dragon`, `fractals.juliacantor`, `fractals.juliapetal`,
  `fractals.logbifur`, `fractals.henon`, `fractals.koch` and `fractals.minkowski`.

- Very minor tweaks to the `gdi.set*` functions.

- Since 7.7.0 `strings.join` could cause segmentation faults when processing tables. This has been fixed.

- Since 7.7.0 the `@`, `$`, `$$` and `$$$` operators could cause segmentation faults when processing large structures.
  This has been fixed, too.

- The sources did not compile in Windows due to a missing `drand48` definition. This has been fixed.

- This release has been successfully Valgrind-tested on x86 Linux.


7.7.5 Ariel, July 13, 2026

- `fractals.mandel` has been extended to generate Julia sets. Just pass a fifth argument for constant c in the
  generating map z -> z^2 + c:

  > fractals.mandel(x, y, maxiter, radius, -0.765!0.12);

- The interface of `fractals.esctime` has been changed and now allows for a lambda parameter, so that you can create
  not only Mandelbrot sets but also Julia sets, for example.

- Fixed various typos in source file comments.


7.7.4 Ariel Update 1, July 12, 2026

- The new function `gdi.plotfn2d` takes a function of two arguments and creates a plot in the 2-dimensional (!)
  plane. The function accepts the same arguments and options as `gdi.plotfn` does, but ignores line-styles.

  Example:

  > import gdi;

  > sombrero := proc(x, y) is
  >    local rho := hypot(x, y);
  >    return if rho = 0 then 1 else 2*besselj(1, Pi*rho)/(Pi*rho) fi
  > end;

  > gdi.plotfn2d(sombrero, -2, 2, -0.2, 1.1, colour = 'maroon');

- Added two new predefined colours to the `gdi` package: 'chartreuse' (yellow-green) and 'spring' (spring green).

- Added new fractals:
  - Julia Cantor set: `fractals.juliacantor`,
  - Julia Petal structure: `fractals.juliapetal`,
  - Logistic bifurcation diagram generator: `fractals.logbifur`,
  - Henon map: `fractals.henon`.

  In your Agena installation folder, see the bottom of the `lib/fractals.agn` package file for one-liners that
  generate the respective plots.

- Added the `evalhf` shortcut to the `maple` package.

- Adapted the Primer and Quick Reference.

- Just download the `agena-7.7.4-update1.zip` file in the Binaries/Agena 7.7.4 folder and check the
  `libupdate.readme` file in the root of the ZIP archive for installation instructions. In short, you'll
  just need to overinstall the files in your local Agena installation folder.


7.7.4 Ariel, July 11, 2026

- `combinat.bell`, `combinat.bernoulli`, `combinat.catalan` and `combinat.euler` can now compute Bell,
  Bernoulli, Catalan and Euler polynomials. Examples:

  > combinat.euler(2, 1.5):
  0.75

  > combinat.bernoulli(4, 0):
  -0.033333333333315

  If you want to define an epsilon bailout value for `combinat.bernoulli` and `combinat.euler`, use the
  new `eps` options. This option is not needed for `combinat.bell` and `combinat.catalan`.

  > combinat.bernoulli(3, Pi, eps = hEps):
  17.772666405461

- Added the `BellB` shortcut for `combinat.bell` to the `maple` package.

- `numarray.sorted`, `stats.fivenum`, `stats.gsmm`, `stats.iqmean`, `stats.percentile`, `stats.prange`,
  `stats.quartiles`, `stats.smm`, `stats.trimean`, `stats.winsor` have been tuned by a tiny bit of 1 %.


7.7.3 Ariel, July 08, 2026

- The new functions `strhash.resize` and `strmap.resize` shrink or expand a hash set or map manually.
  You usually do not need them as the package functions resize a hash set or map automatically. However,
  in order to prevent iterative resizing, you may just define a new size to speed up further operations.

- The new functions `strhash.iterate` and `strmap.iterate` iterate over a hash set or map and return the
  current key (hash sets) or the current key and value (hash maps). Example:

  > h := strmap.new()
  > strmap.include(h, "apple", 10)
  > strmap.include(h, "banana", 20)
  > f := strmap.iterate(h):
  > f():
  apple   10
  > f():
  banana  20
  > f():
  null

- Likewise, new `dblhash.iterate` iterates over number hash tables:

  > d := dblhash.new(1024)
  > for i to 3 do
  >    dblhash.include(d, sin(i))
  > od
  > f := dblhash.iterate(d);
  > f():
  0.8414709848079
  > f():
  0.90929742682568
  > f():
  0.14112000805987
  > f():
  null

- The new `dblmap` package implements number-to-string hash maps, which can store a very large amount óf data
  where the capacity of a table might not suffice.

  > import dblmap
  > h := dblmap.new()
  > dblmap.include(h, 10, 'ten')
  > dblmap.include(h, Pi, 'Pi')
  > dblmap.getitem(h, 10):
  ten
  > dblmap.getitem(h, Pi):
  Pi
  > dblmap.getitem(h, 1):
  null
  > dblmap.purge(h, Pi);
  > dblmap.getitem(h, Pi):
  null
  > dblmap.attrib(h):
  [capacity ~ 8, count ~ 1, empty ~ 7, loadfactor ~ 0.125, utilization ~ 1, void ~ 0]

- Likewise, the new `intmap` package implements integer-to-integer hash maps.

- The Windows installers now include the following latest editions of DLL dependency files: expat-2.8.2,
  freetype-2.14.3, libpng-1.6.58, zlib-1.3.2.

- This release has been Valgrind-checked on x86 Linux to ensure there are no internal errors or memory leaks.


7.7.2 Ariel, July 08, 2026

- `numarray.introsort` has become 19 % faster with integer arrays and 9 % faster with floating-point arrays by
  using the klib library `ksort`.

- The new `strhash` package implements a string hash set, suited for a very large amount of data where
  sets might sooner or later throw out-of-memory errors.

  > import strhash

  > h := strhash.new()

  > strhash.include(h, "apple")
  > strhash.include(h, "banana")
  > strhash.getitem(h, "banana"):
  true

  > strhash.getitem(h, "anana"):
  false

  > strhash.purge(h, "banana");
  > strhash.getitem(h, "banana"):
  false

  > strhash.attrib(h):
  [capacity ~ 8, count ~ 1, empty ~ 7, loadfactor ~ 0.125, utilization ~ 1, void ~ 0]

  > strhash.totable(h):
  [apple]

- Likewise, the new `strmap` package implements a string-to-number hash map, which can also store a
  very large amount of data where the capacity of a table might not suffice.

  > import strmap

  > h := strmap.new()

  > strmap.include(h, "apple", 10)
  > strmap.include(h, "banana", 20)
  > strmap.getitem(h, "banana"):
  20

  > strmap.getitem(h, "anana"):
  null

  > strmap.purge(h, "banana");
  > strmap.getitem(h, "banana"):
  null

  > 10 in h, 10 notin h:
  true    false

  > strmap.attrib(h):
  [capacity ~ 8, count ~ 1, empty ~ 7, loadfactor ~ 0.125, utilization ~ 1, void ~ 0]

  > strmap.totable(h):
  [apple ~ 10]

- This release has been Valgrind-checked on x86 Linux to ensure there are no internal errors or memory leaks.


7.7.1 Ariel, July 06, 2026

- Pure local declarations without associated assignment no longer need to be finalised with a completing semicolon.

  So you can now either write

  > local a, b, c

  or

  > local a, b, c;

  The fix also benefits various flavours of the `local` statement, such as `create local table`, etc.

- Added an extension to the debug library, abci - short for Agena Byte Code Inspector - that wraps various
  `debug` functions used to inspect procedures written in Agena. The package was originally written by
  Luiz Henrique de Figueiredo for Lua 5.1.

  There is no documentation. Check the `lib/abci.agn` file in your Agena installation folder for examples
  instead.

  > import abci

  > f := proc(x, y) is
  >    print(x, '=', y, 23);
  >    local z := x + 2*y;
  >    local last;
  >    X := 245;
  >    return inspect
  > end

  > abci.inspect(debug.getinfo(1).func, true)
        header  procedure(01FC31C0)
        upvalues        0
        source  stdin
        functions       0
        instructions    10
        slots   3
        constants       6
        line    0
        isvararg        true
        locals  0
        params  0
  constants       6
        1       abci
        [...]
  locals  0
  upvalues        0
  functions       0
  instructions    10
       [...]
        4       1       GETTABLE        1       1       -4
        5       1       LOADK   2       -5      null
        6       1       CALL    1       2       2
        7       1       GETTABLE        1       1       -6
        8       1       LOADBOOL        2       1       0
        9       1       CALL    0       3       1
        10      1       RETURN  0       1       null

- The OS/2 WarpIN installer did not create a correct programme folder on the Workplace Shell. This has been fixed.
  The text files linked in the programme folder are now opened by epm to allow a quick view.

  There were similar issues with one of the Windows NSIS installers, which have been corrected, as well.
  The TIFF dependency file has also been removed from all Windows installers as it is not needed at all.


7.7.0 Ariel Update 2, July 05, 2026

- Added further scripts to folder share\scripting:

  - cpuinfo: return data on the installed CPU,
  - drive.agn prints information on a given drive,
  - memory.agn prints information on installed RAM and memory usage.


7.7.0 Ariel Update 1, July 02, 2026

- On Windows, the new field 'usedsize' returned by `os.drivestat` includes the number of occupied bytes, and
  'serialnumber' contains the serial number of the drive. The drive size information is now being determined
  by calling a newer Win32 C API function.

- Various memory leaks have been removed from the RASE application which allows to create an executable binary
