  See Sourceforge download folder `Binaries/Packages`, file `rase.win32-7.7.0.zip`.


7.7.0 Ariel, June 30, 2026

- The `@` mapping operator did not process strings. This has been fixed. The return is a sequence of the mapping
  results.

  Example to convert a string to ASCII codes:

  > <: x -> abs(x) :> @ '12345':
  seq(49, 50, 51, 52, 53)

  The operator has also been tuned by 31 %.

- Likewise, the '$' selection operator now processes strings. Example:

  > s := '01234567890123456789012345678901234567890123456789';

  > f := <: x -> x < '5' :>;

  > f $ s:
  0123401234012340123401234

  The operator has also been tuned by 33 %.

- The `$$` selection and `$$$` counting operators could access invalid memory when under high load. The `$$` operator
  also wrongfully called the `in` metamethod if it was attached to the structure to be scanned. This has all been
  fixed.

  The `$$` and `$$$` operators work with strings now. While `$$` has become 7 % faster, `$$$` has become 2 % slower.

- This release has been Valgrind-checked on x86 and x64 Linux to ensure there are no internal errors or memory leaks.


7.6.10 Dione, June 28, 2026

- New `drop` compliments the `ops` function and drops arguments from the head or tail of an argument list:

  > drop(2, 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'):
  c       d       e

  > drop(-2, 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'):
  a       b       c

  This is useful to avoid creation of dummy variables or skipping excessive arguments manually.

- The new function `net.toip` converts from host name to IPv4 address. The input can be an IP address or host name.

  Example:

  > net.toip('www.lua.org'):
  46.175.8.47     [alias ~ [], ip ~ [46.175.8.47], name ~ www.lua.org]

- New `net.ntohs` converts a non-negative 2-byte integer value from network to host byte order. Likewise, `net.ntohl`
  and `net.htonl` provide 1:1 interfaces to the underlying C functions ntohl() and htonl() for unsigned 4-byte integers.


7.6.9 Dione, June 26, 2026

- You can now use sockets in OS/2. Due to the unique socket management there, with a socket shut down if it is not bound
  or connected immediately, `net.open` has been extended to create a server or client socket and to accept an IP address
  and a port to bind or connect to. This spares you separate calls to `net.bind` or `net.connect` and works with all
  operating systems, not only OS/2, in the very same way:

  > import net;

  > s := net.open(mode = 'client', address = '129.6.15.28', port = 13);  # NIST time server

  > net.receive(s):

  61217 26-06-26 13:59:51 50 0 0 549.9 UTC(NIST) *
        51

  > net.close(s);


7.6.8 Dione, June 25, 2026

- The internal socket management of the `net` package leaked memory, corrupting data about open sockets on the long run.
  This has been fixed.

- The new `net.keep` function sets the keep-alive mode to a socket or deactivates it.


7.6.7 Dione, June 24, 2026

- With table arrays, on average `purge` has become 17 % faster.

- Various underlying sequence and register write operations, as well as the `exchange`, `rotate top`, `rotate bottom`
  statements could destabilize the garbage collector, in the worst case corrupting tables, sequences and registers, at
  least theoretically. This has been fixed.

- Some adaptions to the Linux and Solaris build files to reduce the size of the compiled binaries.

- Removed some unused auxiliary functions from the codebase.

- This release has been Valgrind-checked on x86 and x64 Linux to ensure there are no internal errors or memory leaks.


7.6.6 Dione, June 23, 2026

- Patched Agena's internal termination logic to prevent memory leaks and invalid memory access. This includes a fix to
 `os.exit`. The bugs appeared only when using the srglue and sragena tools, Agena itself had not been affected.

- srglue and sragena have also been patched so that scripts compiled into binaries no longer throw segmentation faults
  in UNIX and Windows. Fixed various bugs and improved error management in the `whereis.agn` test script.

- The `fractals` package did not compile in DOS. This has been fixed.


7.6.5 Dione, June 21, 2026

- Agena now internally prevents a register from being wrongfully shrunk to zero size. If a function or operator tries
  to do this, like `unique` in in-place mode for example, the result will always be a register of size one.

- The Debian x86/x64 and ARM installers no longer issue warnings.

- Removed a few unused C API functions from the codebase.

- This release has been Valgrind-checked on x86 and x64 Linux to ensure there are no internal errors or memory leaks.


7.6.4 Dione, June 20, 2026

- The `unique` function, which removes all duplicates from a table, sequence or register, can now work in-place when
  given the `inplace = true` option, avoiding internal duplication of memory. Example:

  > a := seq(1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 3);

  > unique(a, inplace = true);

  > a:
  seq(1, 2, 3)

- `astro.locate` has been sped up by a factor of at least 2.5 for a hit, and up to 35 for no hit. The function now uses
  a key file for the location data instead of a CSV file and avoids unnecessary lookups.

- `astro.locate` now accepts a specific codepage as an optional argument. In this case, this codepage will be used
  in the search on OS/2, DOS and Windows, instead of the automatically detected codepage. Example:

  > import astro;

  > astro.locate('Lódz', codepage = 852):
  [[country ~ PL, elevation ~ 214, latitude ~ 51.77058, location ~ Lódz, longitude ~ 19.47395,
    population ~ 645693, timezone ~ Europe/Warsaw]]

- If the very last argument to `keyfile.allkeys` is `true`, then each key will be included only once into the result,
  thus ignoring duplicate keys.

- To comply with the Reference, `debug.getlocals`, when given a function, now returns one table of local names and
  thus no longer has multiple returns.

- `calc.savgol` and `calc.savgol64` have been hardened against stack corruption.


7.6.3 Dione, June 18, 2026

- Introduced the new `keyfile` package to create and maintain key files, a specialized database file format designed to
  provide fast, indexed access to data using a key-value architecture.

  In the following example, we create a key file, write data to it, read content from it and finally close it.

  > keyfile.new('test.key');

  > fh := keyfile.open('test.key');

  Put the key file into transaction mode. This is optional:

  > keyfile.start(fh);

  Now add the key-value pairs, one unique key may refer to one or more records:

  > keyfile.write(fh, 'Houston', 'TX');
  > keyfile.write(fh, 'Portland', 'ON');
  > keyfile.write(fh, 'Portland', 'ME');
  > keyfile.write(fh, 'Portland', 'NSW');
  > keyfile.write(fh, 'Chattanooga', 'TN');

  In transaction mode we mark all data to be physically written to the key file later on:

  > keyfile.commit(fh, true);  # pass false to roll back

  Now we flush the data to the file:

  > keyfile.sync(fh);

  Let's iterate over all the records with key 'Portland':

  > index, record := 'Portland', null;

  > while record := keyfile.read(fh, index, record) do
  >    print(index, record)
  > od;
  Portland ON
  Portland ME
  Portland NSW

  > keyfile.close(fh):

- To better protect against the creation of phantom files, `ads.createbase`, `ads.open`, `binio.open`, `io.open`, `gzip.open`,
  `minizip.open`, `xbase.new`, `xbase.open` now validate a given path. The check is not 100 % fail-safe, though.

- In Windows, new `os.getfullpathname` returns the absolute path by calling the Win32 C API function GetFullPathNameW().

- This release has been Valgrind-checked on x86 and x64 Linux to ensure there are no internal errors or memory leaks.


7.6.2 Update 1 Dione, June 11, 2026

- Removed further internal dependencies from the code to facilitate future development. There has been no change at all
  to both functionality and performance. Minor change to the Agena Reference.


7.6.2 Dione, June 10, 2026

- Additional fixes to `io.getclip` and `io.putclip`, which are available in Windows only, significantly reducing errors when
  successively copying and pasting which can cause clipboard race conditions. You can now control how many times the functions
  try to access the Windows clipboard should it be locked, and how many milliseconds to wait between each attempt. Example:

  > io.putclip('agena', wait=100, retries = 1):
  true

- Cleansed the code, removing internal dependencies to make future development a bit easier, especially in UNIX. This also
  resulted in slightly smaller binaries across all platforms.

- This release has been Dr. Memory-checked on Windows and Valgrind-checked on x86 and x64 Linux to ensure there are no internal
  errors or memory leaks.


